{"type": "sena", "labs": [{"stimuli": [{"template_override": 1, "type": "welcome", "_data": [], "contents": [{}], "id": 0}, {"template_override": 1, "_data": [], "type": "audio", "title": "", "contents": [{"type": "slideshow", "value": {"audiosrc": "engenn11le13ob10re2aud01", "coverimg": "engenn11le13ob10re2img01.jpg", "images": [{"image": "engenn11le13ob10re2img01.jpg", "text": "", "id": 0, "time": "0"}, {"image": "engenn11le13ob10re2img01.jpg", "text": "<span class=\"strong notdialogue\">A Clear Conscience</span> </br ></br >Remember that little voice inside your head telling you that you are thinking about doing wrong? It is the voice saying you mustn't do this, you cannot think that or you may not go there. It's trying to keep you out of danger and protect\nyour place in the social environment. How humans came to develop a conscience has been a subject of debate and research at least since Darwin's Origin of Species was published in 1859. Although our inner consciousness may detect something is up, it can't always point us in the right direction. We will choose our own path according to our personal judgment.</p>", "id": 1, "time": "100"}, {"image": "engenn11le13ob10re2img02.jpg", "text": "Having an ethical dilemma means that you are not sure whether there is a \"right\" thing to do in a situation. You are confronted with two imperfect choices, but you will only be able to choose one. How, in this case, do you decide?</p>\nOne technique to use when thinking about a problem is to approach it through utilitarianism. Utilitarianism maintains that the \"right\" choice is the one that does the least harm, focusing on \u201cutility\" or usefulness to society as a whole. The benefits to many, in this argument, far outweigh the benefits to one. Therefore, utilitarians must use this maxim to make their choice. If you are a utilitarian, you will prioritize the consequences over the actions themselves.</p>", "id": 2, "time": "33000"}, {"image": "engenn11le13ob10re2img03.jpg", "text": "Deontology offers another approach. In deontology, actions are either inherently right or wrong. Individuals must distinguish between them. To do this, an individual follows a set of rules. As a follower of deontology, you assess whether the value of a particular action is less than the consequences of that action.\n</p>Utilitarianism and deontology in their purest forms represent the opposite sides of a spectrum. Other theories abound, mixing the two in varying degrees.</p>\nWe  don't have to decide on the \"right\" theory for every situation, but it is interesting to see what insight each perspective has to offer on the path towards a clear conscience.", "id": 3, "time": "72500"}]}}], "id": 1}], "exercises": [{"template_override": 1, "_data": [{"type": "instructions", "contents": [{"content": " I can talk about social issues, beliefs, opinions and moral or personal dilemmas.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": " I can express freedom of choice or prohibition.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": " I can express willingness or intention to do something.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "Have you ever asked yourself: What is the best thing to do? When?", "type": "Free Header Item"}, {"content": " How do you solve moral dilemmas?", "type": "Free Header Item"}]}, {"type": "group", "contents": [{"content": "Welcome", "type": "group_name"}]}, {"content": {"allowed_attempts": 2, "incorrect_feedback": [{"content": "try again", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Maybe next time", "type": "text"}], "correct_feedback": "Correct", "attempt_feedback": [{"content": "You have 2 tries", "type": "text"}, {"content": "you have 1 attempt left", "type": "text"}]}, "type": "feedback"}, {"content": " Ask your partner:", "type": "free_header_text"}, {"content": "Sometimes it's hard to know the right thing to do.", "type": "free_type_text_area"}], "img_path": "", "questions": [], "type": "WLC", "id": 0, "stimuli_ids": [0]}, {"template_override": 1, "_dat