 advice, willingness, or intention to do something in spoken or written texts and in work and social contexts.", "type": "primary"}, {"content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear.", "type": "secondary_1"}, {"content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear.", "type": "secondary_2"}, {"content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear.", "type": "secondary_3"}, {"content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear.", "type": "secondary_4"}, {"content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear.", "type": "secondary_5"}, {"content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear.", "type": "secondary_6"}, {"content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear.", "type": "secondary_7"}, {"content": "Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear.", "type": "secondary_8"}]}, {"content": "engenn11le13ob10re1img01.jpg", "type": "background_image"}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": " Negatives with <span class=\"strong\">Must</span> and <span class=\"strong\">Have to</span>", "type": "heading"}, {"content": " The negatives of <span class=\"strong\">must</span> and <span class=\"strong\">have to</span> have somewhat different meanings than their affirmative counterparts. Let's look at the differences in meanings:", "type": "describer"}, {"content": " <span class=\"strong\">Don't have to</span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "When you want to talk about a situation in which you have the freedom to choose to do something, you use  <span class=\"strong\">don't have to</span>. There is no obligation, you are free to act as you will. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "  For example:<br/>  <span class=\"italic\"> You don't have to attend the company Christmas party.</span> (You can choose to go or not to go.)", "type": "text"}, {"content": " <span class=\"strong\">Must not</span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "When you want to talk about something that is prohibited (strictly forbidden or unacceptable), you use  <span class=\"strong\">must not</span>. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": " For example:<br/>   <span class=\"italic\"> Passengers <span class=\"strong\">must not</span> bring liquids onto the plane.<br/> You <span class=\"strong\">mustn't</span> enter a contract without reading it first.</span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Your conscience is the voice saying you mustn't do this, you cannot think that or you may not go there.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "In deontology, actions are either inherently right or wrong. Individuals must distinguish between them.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "We  don't have to decide on the \"right\" theory for every situation, but it is interesting to see what insight each perspective has to offer.", "type": "example"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": " <span class=\"stronf\">Will</span> and <span class=\"strong\">Would</span> for Willingness and Intention", "type": "heading"}, {"content": " When you want to express intention to do something or a willingness to do something, you can use the modal verbs <span class=\"strong\">will</span> and <span class=\"strong\">would</span>.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "We will choose our own path according to our personal judgment.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "As a follower of deontology, you would assess whether the value of a particular action is less than the consequences of that action.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "If you are a utilitarian, you will prioritize the consequences over the actions themselves.  ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "You can use <span class=\"strong\">will</span> to talk about someone's willingness in the present moment to do something. <BR/> You can use <span class=\"strong\">won't</span> to talk about someone's UNwillingness in the present moment to do something. <BR/> When talking about the past, you can use <span class=\"strong\">would</span> to express that someone was generally willing to do things. <BR/> You can use <span class=\"strong\">wouldn't</span> to talk about UNwillingness in the past in both single situations and generally. <BR/>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Context is particularly important to understand the exact meaning of each verb.", "type": "text"}, {"content": " For example:<br/>   <span class=\"italic\">Joanna <span class=\"strong\">will</span> translate for you - she speaks Japanese. </span>(willingness)<br/>  <span class=\"italic\">I'll do it - I don't mind.</span> (willingness)<br/>  <span class=\"italic\">The workers <span class=\"strong\">won't</span> vote for me. </span>(willingness)<br/>  <span class=\"italic\">I <span class=\"strong\">won't</span> be coming later. </span>(intention)<br/>  <span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\">Would</span> you mind helping me, please? </span>(willingness)<br/>  <span class=\"italic\">I'll call you later. </span>(intention)", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "conscience", "type": "name"}, {"content": "the inner sense of what is right or wrong ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: How humans came to develop a conscience has been a subject of debate and research at least since Darwin's Origin of Species was published in 1859. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn11le13ob10aud01", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word_describer", "contents": [{"content": " These are words you use when you talk about moral dilemmas.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "judgment", "type": "name"}, {"content": "the ability to judge or make decisions", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: We will choose our own path according to our personal judgment.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn11le13ob10aud02", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "imperfect", "type": "name"}, {"content": "not perfect; faulty in some way", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: You are confronted with two imperfect choices, but you will only be able to choose one.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn11le13ob10aud03", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "utilitarianism", "type": "name"}, {"content": "an ethical doctrine based on utility", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: One technique to use when thinking about a problem is to approach it through utilitarianism.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn11le13ob10aud04", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "deontology ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a form of ethics dealing with moral obligation and right action", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: You can approach an ethical dilemma through deontology. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn11le13ob10aud05", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "inherently", "type": "name"}, {"content": " existing within or describing something by its nature ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: In deontology, actions are either inherently right or wrong.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn11le13ob10aud06", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "takeaway_extension", "contents": [{"content": "With a partner, discuss the two ethical doctrines described in this lesson.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Choose a moral dilemma.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Give your opinion on the dilemma.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Using the ethical doctrines, think of at least two different solutions to the moral dilemma.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Write a paragraph of 75 - 100 words summarizing your discussion.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "takeaway_assessment", "contents": [{"content": "I discuss ethical doctrines.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I give my opinion on an ethical dilemma.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I write a paragraph describing solutions to an ethical dilemma.", "type": "text"}]}]}